{
  "abstract": "Tool-using large language model (LLM) agent harnesses depend on Server-Sent Events (SSE) parsers and candidate-selection verifiers whose failure modes are easily conflated with bundled heuristics or sampling noise. Building on close-delimited HTTP/1.0 framing diagnostics (`RFC 9112`) and the terminal-event parser published in `agent_harness_8`, we first isolate what arrival-ordered callback buffering, structured error-code retry classification, nonterminal JSON rejection, and post-retry turn continuation (`ATVS`) add beyond that published parser. Across a 40-run four-mode two-hop proxy and callback-ordering suite, the published parser already resolves mid-reasoning drops, partial multi-tool end-of-file (EOF) cuts, and length truncation, while `ATVS` eliminates uncommitted retry-prefix and content-filter callback leaks, preserves callback arrival order above 512 KiB, separates retryable `server_error` from deterministic `invalid_prompt` failures, rejects `in_progress` JSON, and continues after single-turn retry exhaustion. In a 60-episode counterbalanced `development` trial with zero observed transport faults, `ATVS` scores `64.93%` versus `64.23%` (`+0.70` points, `95% CI [-6.49, +7.89]` at `1.15x` recorded cost), missing our `<= 1.10x` escalation gate without estimating live fault recovery. Second, in candidate-blind workspace adjudication (`BWA`), deterministic vote rescoring across two-replicate 90-pair evaluations shows that abstaining on one-sided `<MISSING>` JSON paths lifts selector score from `57.50%` to `58.89%` (`+1.39` points), whereas broader schema-partitioned packages (`59.17%` and `60.56%`) bundle prompt, context, filtering, and comma-separated value (CSV) budget changes with wide uncertainty. Evaluating the frozen selector across 90 routed pairs from six new same-task trajectories yields `53.06%` (`95% CI [29.74%, 76.38%]`), missing our `63.0%` gate and exposing positional array-index queries on an order-invariant JSON sample set in task `031` (`44.44%`).",
